<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  One shared Keychain group, named explicitly rather than left to the default.

	  The default group is derived from the bundle identifier, so the watch app, which has
	  its own bundle identifier, would look in its own group and find nothing. Naming a
	  group both targets declare is what lets iCloud Keychain carry an account from the
	  phone to the wrist.

	  It has to be set before anyone has accounts, because a Keychain item lives in the
	  group it was written to and moving one afterwards is a migration whose failure mode
	  reads as "my accounts vanished".
	-->
	<!--
	  The share extension's inbox, and nothing else.

	  **An App Group is a grant, not a boundary**, which is the same thing gate E1 proved about
	  Keychain access groups. Nothing here relies on it being private. What lands in it is an
	  image the sender already had, held for seconds, with complete file protection. No key or
	  passphrase material may ever be written here; that is an invariant in docs/VAULT.md.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.dev.openfactor.app</string>
	</array>

	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)dev.openfactor.shared</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
